1
00:00:00,960 --> 00:00:05,600
One rysh session. In a moment, four
Codex agents will go to work inside it.

2
00:00:09,520 --> 00:00:12,000
Three lanes: planning on the left,

3
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:14,960
the builders in the middle,
the shared board on the right.

4
00:00:33,870 --> 00:00:36,030
The roadmap agent goes first,

5
00:00:36,030 --> 00:00:39,150
and its very first act is to
tell the board what this demo is.

6
00:00:45,005 --> 00:00:48,925
Then the other three check in
— standing by, no work yet.

7
00:00:49,005 --> 00:00:52,525
Every one of them will report to
this board from here to the end.

8
00:01:03,920 --> 00:01:08,240
The roadmap writes the goal down: a todo
manager that runs entirely in the browser.

9
00:01:08,465 --> 00:01:11,345
No backend, no build step, no dependencies.

10
00:01:18,945 --> 00:01:21,905
It waits until all three have
checked in before handing over.

11
00:01:22,300 --> 00:01:26,140
A message sent to an agent that
has not booted yet is simply lost.

12
00:01:35,995 --> 00:01:37,675
Handed to the fleet manager.

13
00:01:37,995 --> 00:01:41,515
That is the last order the roadmap
gives — the build is not its job.

14
00:01:52,890 --> 00:01:57,850
The manager splits the work by layer.
Worker one takes the page and the styling,

15
00:01:57,850 --> 00:02:09,670
worker two takes all the behaviour.

16
00:02:19,970 --> 00:02:21,890
But first it writes a contract:

17
00:02:22,130 --> 00:02:26,930
every shared id, the markup of one
todo item, and which file owns what.

18
00:02:27,625 --> 00:02:30,265
Two agents are about to
build one page in parallel,

19
00:02:30,265 --> 00:02:32,505
and this is the only thing
holding them together.

20
00:02:38,105 --> 00:02:42,665
Contract posted. The work orders go out,
one to each worker, and the build starts.

21
00:02:52,040 --> 00:02:53,400
Watch the middle lane.

22
00:02:53,400 --> 00:02:56,680
Both workers are running at once, and
neither has read the other's file.

23
00:03:11,900 --> 00:03:14,140
The board is the only thing they share.

24
00:03:14,220 --> 00:03:18,300
No shared memory, no lock file, no
messages between them — just posts.

25
00:03:33,035 --> 00:03:36,795
Worker two's logic lands first, then
worker one's page and stylesheet.

26
00:03:49,895 --> 00:03:52,215
Both sign off on the board.

27
00:03:52,215 --> 00:03:55,575
Neither reported back by message:
the manager has been watching

28
00:03:55,575 --> 00:03:59,255
the board, because a post cannot
be missed and a message can.

29
00:04:11,910 --> 00:04:14,870
The manager now checks the real
thing rather than the reports.

30
00:04:15,345 --> 00:04:18,705
Does every id the script looks
up actually exist in the markup?

31
00:04:32,960 --> 00:04:37,920
Now the interesting part. Verifying,
the manager finds a real defect — the

32
00:04:37,920 --> 00:04:40,080
remaining count is
written twice on the page.

33
00:04:45,935 --> 00:04:49,855
It does not fix it itself. It names
the mismatch, sends the file back to

34
00:04:49,855 --> 00:04:52,015
worker two, and posts that it is blocked.

35
00:05:00,030 --> 00:05:02,430
Worker two corrects it and signs off again.

36
00:05:02,430 --> 00:05:05,150
The manager re-checks, and
this time everything lines up.

37
00:05:15,955 --> 00:05:19,555
Every shared id matches, both
files load, the syntax is clean.

38
00:05:19,555 --> 00:05:20,755
The manager signs off.

39
00:05:35,905 --> 00:05:40,305
The roadmap checks the finished
app against its own acceptance list

40
00:05:40,305 --> 00:05:43,985
— every action, the live count,
and persistence across a reload.

41
00:06:00,895 --> 00:06:04,815
Four agents, one board, a working
app. And all four have signed off.

